{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "self_join_index_set_key_ambiguous",
    "description": "INVALID: an `on` key NAMES an index set that more than one of the node's ranges draws, and the clause carries no `syms` (CONFORMANCE_SPEC.md §5.5.8).\n\n`a` and `b` both draw `{from: rows}`, and the left key is the literal string `rows` -- the index set itself. Unlike a DATA COLUMN, whose axis is a property of the column and not of the clause, a key naming an index set can already say which side it means by naming the RANGE SYMBOL instead: `[\"a\", \"row_id\"]`. So the default side assignment deliberately does NOT apply here, at two candidates or at any number, and this stays a rejection. Adding the self-join capability must not remove a diagnostic: were the default applied, the left key would silently resolve to `a`, the pair would compare `a`'s key with itself, and the clause would drop as a tautology -- an ungated 25-term product reported as a number.\n\nThe defect is decidable STATICALLY from this ONE document -- the node's `ranges`, the document `index_sets`, the declared variable shapes and the clause itself are all here; no evaluation, no solver, no runtime data, no other file. `validate()` MUST reject it, in every binding, which is why it is pinned as a STRUCTURAL finding in tests/invalid/expected_errors.json rather than left to whichever pass a given implementation happens to run. The document is SCHEMA-VALID: the schema cannot express \"this key's axis is drawn by more than one range\".",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "aggregate",
      "join",
      "self-join",
      "invalid",
      "build-time",
      "ambiguous-side"
    ]
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 5
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "IndexSetKeyAmbiguous": {
      "variables": {
        "row_id": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "The relation's key column."
        },
        "row_prior": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "rows"
          ],
          "description": "A shifted copy of `row_id` -- an ordinary 1-D data column over the SAME index set, which is what makes the clause a self-join."
        },
        "count": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Counts the admitted pairs. Never reached: the document is rejected."
        }
      },
